  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout danger">ANCHOR POSTER: Alongside §§252–256a (measurement), this Titel carries the heaviest substantive weight in all of HGB's individual-accounts provisions. §249's Rückstellungen concept in particular is recognised more LIBERALLY than IAS 37's own provisions concept, and §246(1) sentence 4's mandatory goodwill amortisation stands in direct, genuine conflict with IFRS 3/IAS 36's impairment-only model. Expect real depth below.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Deciding WHAT belongs on the balance sheet — the question that precedes measurement</div>
      <div class="principle">§§246–251 answer the RECOGNITION question — what must, may, or may NOT appear as an asset or liability at all — BEFORE any question of HOW MUCH it should be measured at (that comes next, in §§252–256a). This Titel houses two of HGB's most distinctive, prudence-driven concepts: the MANDATORY, closed-list Rückstellungen regime (§249), and the specifically named Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten category (§250) — both broader or more mechanically distinctive than their nearest IFRS counterparts.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§246–251 MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Vollständigkeitsgebot (completeness requirement)</strong> — §246(1) demands EVERY asset, liability, expense, and income item appear, unless the law itself creates an exception</span>
        <span><strong>Vorsichtsprinzip / Imparitätsprinzip</strong> — §249's mandatory provisioning for impending losses is the CLEAREST possible expression of asymmetric, loss-anticipating prudence anywhere in HGB</span>
        <span><strong>Realisationsprinzip</strong> — §250's RAP mechanism exists PRECISELY to keep expenses and income matched to the correct period, deferring recognition until the period they actually belong to</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§246–251 require, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core requirement</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>246</span><span>Completeness; no offsetting (with one mandatory exception); mandatory goodwill amortisation</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>247</span><span>Fixed vs current asset split, tested by intended continuing use</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>248</span><span>Founding/equity-raising costs never capitalised; self-created intangibles a genuine election (with exceptions)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>249</span><span>Mandatory, closed-list provisions — uncertain liabilities, onerous contracts, Kulanz, deferred maintenance</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>250</span><span>Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten — a distinctly named accrual/deferral balance sheet category</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>251</span><span>Below-the-line disclosure of guarantees and similar contingent liabilities</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every Kaufmann preparing a Jahresabschluss under §§242–245</div>
      <div class="principle">§§246–251 apply to every Kaufmann required to prepare a Jahresabschluss under the preceding Titel — the recognition rules are GENERAL provisions, applicable regardless of legal form, though certain specific line items (e.g. the Sonderposten für allgemeine Bankrisiken referenced in §247(3)) are only ever relevant to credit institutions covered separately in Book 3, Section 4.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§246(1) — the Completeness Principle</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Every asset, liability, RAP, expense, and income item — unless the law says otherwise</div>
      <div class="principle">§246(1) sentence 1 requires the Jahresabschluss to include ALL assets, liabilities, Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten, expenses, and income — UNLESS the law itself permits an exception. This is a genuinely strong presumption: silence or ambiguity is resolved in favour of INCLUSION, not exclusion, and any departure from completeness requires a POSITIVE legal basis rather than merely being left unaddressed.</div>
      <div class="callout info">§246(1) sentence 2 additionally attributes assets to their ECONOMIC (not merely legal) owner — the concept explored in Section 4.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Wirtschaftliches Eigentum — Economic Ownership (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Substance over legal form, German-style</div>
      <div class="principle">§246(1) sentence 2 requires assets to be attributed to the OWNER — but where another party effectively holds the substantive economic power over an asset (excludes the legal owner from the ordinary exercise of ownership rights over its ECONOMIC useful life), that OTHER party recognises the asset instead. This "wirtschaftliches Eigentum" doctrine is HGB's own, judicially-developed version of substance-over-form — conceptually similar to, but historically and doctrinally distinct from, IFRS's control-based recognition tests.</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A retention-of-title sale (Eigentumsvorbehalt)</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A manufacturer sells machinery to a customer under a standard retention-of-title clause (Eigentumsvorbehalt) — LEGAL title remains with the seller until the final instalment is paid, but the customer takes immediate possession, bears all risk of loss, and uses the machinery in its own production process from delivery onward.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the seller retaining legal title, the CUSTOMER recognises the machinery as its own asset from the point of delivery — economic ownership (the substantive power to use the asset and bear its risks) has passed, and §246(1) sentence 2 looks to THAT, rather than to the formal legal title the retention-of-title clause preserves.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>THE GOODWILL RULE — Mandatory Amortisation, Not Impairment-Only</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A direct, genuine conflict with the IFRS/US GAAP impairment-only model</div>
      <div class="principle">§246(1) sentence 4 deems goodwill arising on a business combination (der Geschäfts- oder Firmenwert) to be a "zeitlich begrenzt nutzbarer Vermögensgegenstand" — an asset with a LIMITED useful life. This single sentence has an enormous practical consequence: goodwill under HGB MUST be systematically AMORTISED over its estimated useful life, exactly like any other depreciable fixed asset. There is NO impairment-only alternative available — a company cannot elect to simply test goodwill for impairment each year the way IFRS 3/IAS 36 require internationally, and the way even ASPE offers only as an ALTERNATIVE (see the earlier companion poster on ASPE Section 1582) rather than the SOLE mandated approach HGB imposes.</div>
      <div class="callout warn">Where the useful life cannot be reliably estimated, established German commentary and practice generally look to a period of around 10 years (paralleling, coincidentally, the same 10-year ceiling ASPE applies to its OWN optional goodwill amortisation alternative) — though the amortisation period must still be individually justified and disclosed in the Anhang, not simply defaulted to.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Amortising Purchased Goodwill</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A straightforward, mandatory, systematic write-off</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Amortising goodwill from an acquisition</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A GmbH acquires a competitor for €4,200,000, with the acquiree's identifiable net assets valued at €3,000,000. Management estimates a useful life of 8 years for the resulting goodwill, being unable to justify a longer period given the nature of the acquired customer relationships.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Goodwill = €4,200,000 − €3,000,000 = <strong>€1,200,000</strong>, amortised on a straight-line basis at €1,200,000 ÷ 8 = <strong>€150,000 per year</strong> — a MANDATORY annual expense, REGARDLESS of whether the acquired business is performing well, since HGB's goodwill rule is not conditioned on any impairment indicator, unlike an impairment-only regime.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§246(2) — the Prohibition on Offsetting, and Its One Mandatory Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The general rule: gross presentation, always</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§246(2) sentence 1 prohibits offsetting assets against liabilities, expenses against income, and land rights against building charges — the DEFAULT posture is always GROSS presentation, preserving the completeness principle's substantive effect rather than allowing net presentation to quietly obscure it.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cw">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The one MANDATORY exception: pension plan assets</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§246(2) sentence 2 — introduced by the 2009 BilMoG reform — REQUIRES plan assets held exclusively to settle pension or similar long-term employee benefit obligations, and insulated from all other creditors, to be OFFSET against the corresponding provision. This is not a permission but a MANDATE — the only place in §246(2) where netting is not merely allowed but actually required, conceptually paralleling how IAS 19 nets plan assets against the defined benefit obligation internationally.</div>
      <div class="callout suc">A genuine point of convergence with IFRS, introduced specifically to modernise HGB's treatment of funded pension arrangements.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§247 — Content of the Balance Sheet; the "Dedication to Continuing Use" Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A fundamentally different classification test than IAS 1's own</div>
      <div class="principle">§247(1) requires fixed assets (Anlagevermögen) and current assets (Umlaufvermögen) to be shown SEPARATELY, with sufficient breakdown. §247(2) then supplies the ACTUAL legal test: only those assets DESTINED to serve the business on a CONTINUING basis ("dazu bestimmt sind, dauernd dem Geschäftsbetrieb zu dienen") count as Anlagevermögen. This is an INTENT-based test, hinging on management's PURPOSE for holding the asset — genuinely different in character from IAS 1's own current/non-current test, which instead asks whether realisation is EXPECTED within twelve months or the entity's normal operating cycle.</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB §247(2)</span><span>IAS 1</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Classification test</span><span>Management's INTENDED continuing use (a purpose-based test)</span><span>Expected REALISATION within 12 months / operating cycle (a timing-based test)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>An asset held indefinitely but with no fixed timing</span><span>Anlagevermögen, GIVEN the continuing-use intent</span><span>Requires an explicit timing judgement regardless of intent</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Classifying an Asset Under §247(2)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">The same physical asset, two different classifications, depending on purpose</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A vehicle held for two different purposes</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Company A, a logistics firm, purchases a delivery van intended to be used in its own delivery operations for the foreseeable future. Company B, a used-vehicle dealership, purchases an identical van purely to resell it to a customer within the next few weeks.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Company A classifies the van as <strong>Anlagevermögen</strong> — it is DEDICATED to continuing use in the business, regardless of its actual expected holding period. Company B classifies the SAME van as <strong>Umlaufvermögen</strong> — it is held for RESALE, not continuing operational use, DESPITE being a physically identical asset. The classification turns entirely on the HOLDER'S purpose, not the asset's own characteristics.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>§248 — Recognition Prohibitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Founding costs and equity-raising costs: never an asset</div>
      <div class="principle">§248(1) prohibits capitalising expenditure incurred in FOUNDING the enterprise (Aufwendungen für die Gründung des Unternehmens) or in PROCURING equity capital (Aufwendungen für die Beschaffung des Eigenkapitals) — these must be expensed immediately, REGARDLESS of any argument that they generate future economic benefit. This reflects HGB's characteristically strict, prudence-first posture toward intangible or uncertain future benefits.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>§248(2) — the Self-Created Intangibles Election (a Rare Point of IFRS Alignment)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuine capitalisation election — introduced by BilMoG 2009 — with the same excluded categories IAS 38 also excludes</div>
      <div class="principle">§248(2) sentence 1 permits an enterprise to ELECT to capitalise self-created intangible FIXED assets (selbst geschaffene immaterielle Vermögensgegenstände des Anlagevermögens) — a genuine accounting POLICY choice, introduced specifically by the 2009 BilMoG reform to modernise HGB's treatment of internally developed intangibles (e.g. internally developed software or technology). However, §248(2) sentence 2 PERMANENTLY excludes self-created BRANDS, mastheads/imprints (Drucktitel), publishing titles, customer lists, and comparable intangible assets from capitalisation, REGARDLESS of the general election.</div>
      <div class="callout suc">Interestingly, this SPECIFIC exclusion list closely mirrors IAS 38.63's own prohibition on capitalising internally generated brands, mastheads, publishing titles, customer lists, and similar items — a genuine, if narrow, point of SUBSTANTIVE convergence between HGB and IFRS, rather than the divergence we see almost everywhere else in this Titel.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>§249 — Rückstellungen: THE Central Recognition Concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Mandatory provisioning for uncertain liabilities and impending losses</div>
      <div class="principle">§249(1) sentence 1 REQUIRES provisions for (1) uncertain liabilities (ungewisse Verbindlichkeiten) — obligations that certainly or probably exist, but whose amount or precise trigger remains uncertain — and (2) impending losses from executory/pending contracts (drohende Verluste aus schwebenden Geschäften), essentially an onerous-contract provision. §249(1) sentence 2 EXTENDS this mandatory list to several further named categories, discussed in Section 13.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>The Closed List (Numerus Clausus) of Permitted Provisions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">§249(2): only the cases the law itself names</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§249(2) states plainly that provisions may ONLY be recognised for the cases named in paragraph 1 or elsewhere in the law — a genuine NUMERUS CLAUSUS (closed list), in sharp contrast to IAS 37's more OPEN, principles-based approach built around a single general test (a present obligation, arising from a past event, with a probable outflow, reliably estimable).</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cw">
      <div class="ctitle cw">§249(1) sentence 2's specific mandatory categories</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>Deferred maintenance (unterlassene Instandhaltung) caught up within the FIRST THREE MONTHS of the following financial year</span>
        <span>Warranty obligations granted WITHOUT any specific legal or contractual obligation to do so (Kulanzrückstellungen — Section 15)</span>
        <span>Removal, dismantling, and restoration obligations of a similar character</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Worked Example — an Onerous Contract Provision</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A loss-making executory contract, provisioned in full immediately</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: A supply contract that has turned loss-making</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A manufacturer commits to supplying components to a customer at a fixed price of €500,000 under a three-year contract. Following a sharp, sustained rise in raw material costs, management now estimates the total cost of fulfilling the remaining contract will be €580,000.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The company recognises a Rückstellung of €580,000 − €500,000 = <strong>€80,000</strong> IMMEDIATELY, for the full anticipated loss on the remaining executory contract — CONSISTENT with §249(1) sentence 1's mandatory treatment of impending losses, and with HGB's characteristic Imparitätsprinzip (recognising anticipated losses in full and immediately, well before they are actually realised).</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Kulanzrückstellungen — Provisions With No Legal Obligation At All</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Arguably broader than even IAS 37's constructive obligation test</div>
      <div class="principle">A "Kulanzrückstellung" is a provision for warranty-style obligations the enterprise has NO legal or contractual duty to honour, but which it nonetheless customarily DOES honour, as a matter of established business practice (Handelsbrauch) — for instance, replacing a product just outside its formal warranty period because doing so has become the enterprise's own settled custom. IAS 37 permits provisions for CONSTRUCTIVE obligations, but only where the enterprise's own past practice has created a VALID EXPECTATION in other parties that it will discharge the obligation — HGB's Kulanz concept rests on a SOMEWHAT looser foundation still, anchored in customary business practice generally, and is explicitly NAMED and MANDATED by statute (§249(1) sentence 2) rather than derived from a general obligation-based test the way IAS 37's constructive obligation category is.</div>
      <div class="callout warn">This is one of the clearest illustrations in all of HGB of how far the Vorsichtsprinzip pushes recognition BEYOND what a strict, present-obligation-based IFRS analysis would ever require.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Worked Example — a Goodwill Warranty Provision (Kulanzrückstellung)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Recognising an obligation the company is not legally bound to honour</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 5: A consumer electronics company's informal warranty extensions</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A consumer electronics company's formal written warranty runs for 24 months. In practice, over many years, the company has consistently and routinely replaced defective units for customers up to 30 months after purchase, as an unwritten but well-established customer service practice. Based on historical claims data, management estimates €65,000 of expected replacement costs relating to units sold this year that will fall within this informal 24–30 month window next year.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE having NO legal or contractual obligation extending beyond the formal 24-month warranty, the company recognises a Kulanzrückstellung of <strong>€65,000</strong> — its OWN settled business practice of honouring these informal claims is sufficient, under §249(1) sentence 2, to MANDATE a provision.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Worked Example — the Deferred Maintenance Catch-Up Provision</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Maintenance skipped this year, but caught up early next year, is still THIS year's cost</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 6: Postponed factory maintenance</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A manufacturing company postpones scheduled factory maintenance originally planned for December (this fiscal year), instead completing the work in late February of the following year — within the first three months of the new fiscal year — at a cost of €95,000.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Because the maintenance is carried out within the FIRST THREE MONTHS of the following year, the company recognises a Rückstellung of <strong>€95,000</strong> in the CURRENT year's Jahresabschluss — matching the maintenance cost to the year the underlying wear and deferred obligation actually relate to, RATHER than to the year the cash is ultimately spent.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>§249(3) — Reversal of Provisions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Reversed only to the extent the underlying reason has genuinely disappeared</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§249(3) — actually the successor provision after BilMoG's 2009 restructuring, now embedded within the broader §249 framework — requires that a provision be reversed ONLY to the extent that the reason for recognising it no longer exists, CONCEPTUALLY parallel to IAS 37's own reversal test. A provision cannot simply be released because management has changed its mind; the underlying uncertain liability or impending loss must have genuinely resolved, reduced, or ceased to exist.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>§250 — Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten (RAP)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A distinctly named, legally defined balance sheet category IFRS simply embeds elsewhere</div>
      <div class="principle">§250(1) requires an ASSET-side RAP where expenditure incurred BEFORE the balance sheet date represents an expense for a period AFTER it (e.g. rent paid in advance). §250(2) mirrors this for the LIABILITY side — income received before the balance sheet date that belongs to a period after it. Unlike IFRS, which simply folds these amounts into ordinary "prepayments" or "deferred income" without a distinctly named legal category, HGB's §266 balance sheet format PRESCRIBES "Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten" as its OWN, separately labelled line item on both sides of the Bilanz.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 20 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s20"><span class="num">20</span><h2>Worked Example — the Damnum/Disagio Treatment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A named, specific provision for a loan discount, distinct from IFRS's net-carrying-amount approach</div>
      <div class="principle">§250(3) permits the difference between a loan's nominal repayment amount and the (lower) amount actually disbursed to the borrower — the Damnum or Disagio — to be capitalised as an ACTIVE RAP item, and written off over the term of the loan, RATHER than being expensed immediately.</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 7: Recognising and amortising a Damnum</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A company takes out a €1,000,000 loan with a 5-year term, but the bank disburses only €960,000 to the company, retaining €40,000 as a Damnum. The full €1,000,000 must still be repaid at maturity.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The company recognises the €40,000 Damnum as an ACTIVE Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten, amortising it at €40,000 ÷ 5 = <strong>€8,000 per year</strong> over the loan's term — ECONOMICALLY similar in substance to how IFRS's effective interest method would spread the SAME discount through amortised cost, but STRUCTURALLY different in presentation: HGB shows a separate, named RAP asset, whereas IFRS simply reduces the loan's own net carrying amount directly, with no distinct RAP-style line item at all.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 21 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s21"><span class="num">21</span><h2>§251 — Haftungsverhältnisse (Below-the-Line Contingent Liabilities)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Disclosed, not recognised — a structural point of alignment with IAS 37</div>
      <div class="principle">§251 requires DISCLOSURE (either below the balance sheet line itself, or in the Anhang) of specific contingent liability categories NOT recognised as a Rückstellung on the balance sheet: liabilities from the issuance and transfer of bills of exchange (Wechsel); liabilities from guarantees, bill-of-exchange and cheque guarantees, and warranty contracts (Bürgschaften, Wechsel- und Scheckbürgschaften, Gewährverträge); and liabilities from providing collateral for third-party debts.</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>These items are, BY DEFINITION, ones that have NOT met the recognition threshold for a Rückstellung under §249 — if the probability of outflow becomes sufficiently likely, the item MOVES from §251 disclosure into a recognised §249 liability instead</span>
        <span>This disclosed-but-not-recognised structure closely PARALLELS IAS 37's own treatment of contingent liabilities internationally — a genuine point of structural alignment, even where the SPECIFIC named categories differ</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 22 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s22"><span class="num">22</span><h2>Worked Example — Disclosing a Guarantee Under §251</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A guarantee that has not (yet) crossed into recognition</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 8: Guaranteeing a subsidiary's bank loan</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent company guarantees a €2,000,000 bank loan taken out by its subsidiary. The subsidiary is currently performing well, and management assesses the likelihood of the parent ever having to honour the guarantee as low, though not remote enough to ignore entirely.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The parent discloses the FULL €2,000,000 guarantee below the balance sheet line (or in the Anhang) under §251 — NO liability is recognised on the face of the Bilanz, since the probability of an actual outflow does not yet meet the §249 recognition threshold. Should the subsidiary's financial position later deteriorate such that a claim becomes LIKELY, the guarantee would need to be RECLASSIFIED from §251 disclosure into a recognised §249 Rückstellung.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 23 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s23"><span class="num">23</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether an informal warranty practice has genuinely become established enough (Handelsbrauch) to trigger a mandatory Kulanzrückstellung</span>
            <span>Estimating goodwill's useful life where no specific, reliable estimate is readily available</span>
            <span>Whether a specific asset is genuinely "dedicated to continuing use" under §247(2), particularly for dual-purpose or transitional assets</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Distinguishing the point at which a §251 disclosed contingency has become likely enough to require §249 recognition instead</span>
            <span>Applying the three-month deferred-maintenance catch-up test where the maintenance work spans the cutoff date itself</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 24 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s24"><span class="num">24</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IAS 37, IAS 38, IFRS 3)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — HGB §§246–251 vs IAS 37 / IAS 38 / IFRS 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB</span><span>IFRS</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Goodwill subsequent treatment</span><span>MANDATORY amortisation (§246(1) s.4)</span><span>Impairment-only (IAS 36/IFRS 3) — no amortisation permitted</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Provisions recognition structure</span><span>Closed, statutory list (numerus clausus, §249(2))</span><span>Open, principles-based general test (IAS 37)</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Provisions for customary, non-legal obligations</span><span>Explicitly MANDATED (Kulanzrückstellungen)</span><span>Only via the narrower constructive obligation test</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Self-created brand/masthead/customer-list capitalisation</span><span>PROHIBITED (§248(2) s.2)</span><span>PROHIBITED (IAS 38.63) — a rare point of convergence</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Contingent liability disclosure (not recognised)</span><span>§251, below the line or in Anhang</span><span>IAS 37 disclosure — structurally similar</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This Titel shows HGB's relationship with IFRS at its most VARIED — sitting somewhere between direct, substantive CONFLICT (goodwill amortisation), genuinely BROADER recognition (Kulanzrückstellungen), and occasional, narrow ALIGNMENT (the self-created intangibles exclusion list) — rather than any single, consistent pattern of divergence or convergence.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 25 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s25"><span class="num">25</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§246–251 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Goodwill amortisation (§246(1) s.4)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">GuV</span></span><span>Mandatory, systematic annual expense</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Rückstellung recognised (§249)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">GuV</span></span><span>Liability and matching expense recognised together</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>RAP recognised (§250)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span></span><span>Deferred expense/income moved to the correct period</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Haftungsverhältnisse (§251)</span><span><span class="badge be">Anhang</span></span><span>Disclosure only — no Bilanz recognition</span></div>
